==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|ataindre}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|attingō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/əˈteɪn/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-attain.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪn|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[accomplish]]; to [[achieve]].
#: ''To '''attain''' such a high level of proficiency requires hours of practice each day.''
# To get at the knowledge of; to ascertain.
#* Fuller
#*: not well '''attaining''' his meaning
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To reach or come to, by progression or motion; to arrive at.
#* Milton
#*: Canaan he now '''attains'''.
#* Bible, Psalms cxxxix. 6
#*: Such knowledge is too wonderful for me; it is high, I can not '''attain''' unto it.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come or arrive, by motion, growth, bodily exertion, or efforts toward a place, object, state, etc.; to reach.
#* Bible, Acts xxvii. 12
#*: if by any means they might '''attain''' to Phenice
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: Nor nearer might the dogs '''attain'''.
#* Cowper
#*: to see your trees '''attain''' to the dignity of timber
#* J. R. Green
#*: Few boroughs had as yet '''attained''' to power such as this.
# To reach in excellence or degree; to [[equal]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[overtake]].
#: {{rfquotek|Francis Bacon}}

